description:
Before loading a DLL from a plugin folder, a program checks the path it is about to use. Write

```cpp
std::string normalizePath(const std::string& path);
bool safePluginPath(const std::string& pluginFolder, const std::string& candidate);
```

`normalizePath` cleans up a Windows path:

- `/` becomes `\`; repeated backslashes become one; a trailing backslash is removed (except right after a drive, `C:\`);
- `.` parts are dropped and `..` removes the part before it. A `..` that would go above the drive (or above the start of a relative path) makes the whole result empty, `""`.

`safePluginPath(folder, candidate)` is `true` only if:

- `candidate` is **absolute**: it starts with a drive letter, a colon and a backslash or slash (`C:\...`);
- after normalising both, the candidate is **inside** the folder: it starts with the folder followed by `\` (comparison ignores upper and lower case, as Windows does);
- its file name ends with `.dll` (any case).

```
normalizePath("C:\\Games//Xenon\\plugins\\.\\fog\\..\\fog.dll") == "C:\\Games\\Xenon\\plugins\\fog.dll"
safePluginPath("C:\\Games\\Xenon\\plugins", "C:\\Games\\Xenon\\plugins\\..\\..\\Windows\\evil.dll") == false
```

